window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2022-12-3", "source": "2022年12月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence“Today increasing importance is being attached to cultivating college students'team spirit.” You can make statements, give reasons, or cite examples to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "Today increasing importance is being attached to cultivating college students' team spirit. Both universities and\nfuture employers have realized that few achievements today are the work of a single hand, so this quality truly\ndeserves our attention.\nThere are convincing reasons behind this trend. First, almost every modern task, from a laboratory experiment\nto a business start-up, demands that people with different strengths pool their efforts; without cooperation, even\ntalented individuals can accomplish little. Second, working in a team teaches students to communicate\npatiently, respect different opinions, and share responsibility, all of which are qualities that textbooks alone can\nnever provide. A student who once relied only on personal effort, for instance, may finally learn to trust others\nafter completing a group project together.\nFor these reasons, team spirit should never be treated as something optional. Colleges ought to design more\ngroup tasks, and students themselves should step out of their comfort zones and embrace cooperation, because\nit is teamwork that turns scattered abilities into real strength."}, "translation": {"prompt": "云贵高原(the Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau)大部分位于云南、贵州省境内，总面积约50万平方公里，平均海拔2000-4000米，是中国第四大高原。云贵高原西高东低，河流众多，形成了许多又深又陡的峡谷(canyon)。 峡谷中许多地方土壤肥沃，非常有利于多种农作物生长。 云贵高原独特的自然环境造就了生物和文化的多样性。它是中国森林和矿产资源类型十分丰富的地区，也是古人类起源的重要地区。云贵高原是中国少数民族数量最多的地区，各民族都保留了自己丰富多彩的文化传统。", "sample": "Located mostly in Yunnan Province and Guizhou Province, the Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau, with a total area of about 500,000 square kilometers and an average altitude of 2,000 to 4,000 meters, is the fourth largest plateau in China. High in the west and low in the east, the plateau is crisscrossed by numerous rivers, which have carved out many deep and steep canyons. In many parts of these canyons, the soil is fertile, which is highly favorable for the growth of various crops. Such a unique natural environment has given rise to rich biological and cultural diversity. It is a region rich in various types of forest and mineral resources in China, and it is also an important area for the origin of ancient humans. The Yunnan-Guizhou Plateau is home to the largest number of ethnic minorities in China, where each ethnic group has preserved its own rich and colorful cultural traditions."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "American colleges and universities are using 64 percent less coal than they did a decade ago, burning 700,000 tons last year, down from 2 million tons in 2008, the U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA) said in a report  __26__  yesterday.\nAll 57 schools that were burning coal in 2008 are using less now, and 20 have  __27__  coal completely, EIA found.\nMost universities have turned to natural gas as a  __28__  , with state funding backing the fuel switch.\nWhile academic institutions use less than 0.1 percent of U.S. coal burned for power, campus coal use has a history dating back to the 1800s when  __29__  to power was scarce.\nMany universities still operate their own power plants. The Public Utility Regulatory Policies Act of 1978 encouraged more electricity generation by allowing institutions to sell  __30__  power to utilities.\nBut EIA noted many coal-fired universities have signed onto the American College and University Presidents Climate Commitment, which was launched in 2007.\nAbout 665 schools are part of the program, which aims to  __31__  greenhouse gas emissions. Thirty percent of the participants have pledged to be carbon  __32__  within 20 years.\nThe Sierra Club's Beyond Coal campaign, which also leads campaigns for universities to withdraw their  __33__  in coal and other fossil fuels, lists 22 schools that have pledged to move \"beyond coal,\" including Clemson University, Indiana University, Ohio University, Penn State University, the University of Louisville and the University of Tennessee, Knoxville.\nThe largest coal use  __34__  at colleges were in Michigan, Missouri, Tennessee and Indiana. Indiana's universities alone cut coal  __35__  by 81 percent between 2008 and 2015.\nDuring the same period, Michigan made an 80 percent cut and Tennessee cut back by 94 percent at state institutions.", "matchIntro": "Classical music aims to evolve, build audiences without alienating old guard", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "In 1913, classical music sparked a riot in Paris. Igor Stravinsky was introducing his revolutionary \"Rite of Spring\" ballet to the world, with its discordant melodies and unorthodox choreography(编舞), and the purists in the crowd expressed their disapproval loud and clear. It might have been classical music's version of the time Bob Dylan went electric at the Newport Folk Festival. \"The noise, fighting, and shouting in the audience got so loud,\" NPR's music reporter Miles Hoffman said of the Stravinsky debut, \"that the choreographer had to shout out the numbers to the dancers so that they knew what they were supposed to do.\""}, {"label": "B", "text": "It's difficult to imagine a similar disturbance occurring today within America's sacred symphony halls. In fact, it's hard to picture any kind of disruptive activity at all (unless someone's cell phone happens to go off, and then you'd better watch your back). A mannerly aura(氛围) hangs over most classical proceedings, and many of the genre's biggest supporters would have it no other way."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Today, Western audiences for classical music and opera and ballet are almost always well dressed, older, respectful, achingly silent and often very wealthy (one has to be able to afford most tickets). But as many of America's most storied \"highbrow\"(高雅的) institutions struggle financially—the Philadelphia Orchestra's much-publicized rebound from bankruptcy is just one recent example—classical music fans and theorists are wondering how the medium can weave itself into the 21st century's cultural fabric without sacrificing its integrity."}, {"label": "D", "text": "For example, should we feel OK \"clapping\" during classical music events, even if nobody else is? Why shouldn't we cheer for something great, like we do at a rock concert? The Huffington Post recently ran a Great Debate on this issue and many commenters came out on the side of silence. \"There is no more rewarding experience in life than being part of an audience where everybody is leaning forward in silence, thoroughly carried away by a great performance of a masterpiece,\" one commenter wrote. \"Why is it so difficult for folks to develop an appreciation and understanding for the mannerisms and traditions of classical music?\" asked another."}, {"label": "E", "text": "The truth is that classical music audiences weren't always so polite. Robert Greenberg, an award-winning composer, said that when Beethoven first performed his 7th Symphony, audiences forced the orchestra to perform encores(重演) of certain movements immediately, applauding wildly. And in the last few decades, he said, many audiences at opera performances have abandoned pretenses, yelling \"Bravo\" when they feel like it."}, {"label": "F", "text": "\"I don't think there's anything wrong with an audience showing their enthusiasm for a proper moment by applauding, showing their joy,\" Greenberg said, noting that the stuffiness in concert halls is \"one aspect of contemporary concert etiquette\" he doesn't understand. \"Instead of waiting half an hour to show enthusiasm, why not show it every eight or nine minutes?\""}, {"label": "G", "text": "Until the rules about behavior and clothing change, it's hard to imagine multitudes of young people filling concert halls on their own accord. They're probably more likely to head to Central Park to watch a free performance with a bottle of wine and their friends. \"I think anyone should be able to come into a performance dressed any way they like, and be comfortable any way they like, sitting in that seat ready to enjoy themselves,\" Greenberg said. \"Because it's enjoyable.\""}, {"label": "H", "text": "Greenberg stressed that he doesn't want people to start respecting the music less, and he's not suggesting that we \"dumb down\" the experience. Rather, it's about opening up \"access.\" When operas first instituted subtitles(字幕) during shows, he said, many purists didn't like the idea, believing that the audience should instead study the works before attending. But now it's commonplace to find titles on the seatback in front of you—choose a language, sit back, and understand what's going on."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Allison Vulgamore, president of the Philadelphia Orchestra, is certainly looking to the future. She says certain \"classics concerts\" dedicated to the old masters will always exist, but not every program has to feature Beethoven and Brahms—or even a stage and seats. \"We're trying to introduce different kinds of concerts in different ways,\" she said. \"We are an interactive society now, where people like to learn.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "As the Philadelphia Orchestra rebounds from its financial straits, it is also aiming to experiment, without alienating the loyalists. Vulgamore pointed to Cirque de la Symphonie, a recent offering in which jugglers(玩杂耍的人) and acrobats(杂技演员) interacted with musicians. An upcoming collaboration with New York City's Ridge Theatre, meanwhile, will feature a \"suspended dance installation\" and other theatrical elements occurring in conjunction with an orchestral piece."}, {"label": "K", "text": "The orchestra also continues to offer $25 annual memberships to Philadelphia students, who can buy rush tickets to every concert on the schedule. \"Students line up for the concerts they want, and we get roughly 300 or 350 kids a night coming to these. They take any of the open seats available, 5 minutes before the concert starts,\" Vulgamore said. \"It's like the running of the bulls, that energy when the doors open.\""}, {"label": "L", "text": "Greenberg thinks that youthful energy needs to be harvested. Conductors don't have to be arrogant and untouchable—they can be accessible. Perhaps there could even be a \"bit of humor\" about them, he suggested, and an abandoning of pretension within the high-art institutions themselves. \"On one hand, these organizations are all saying the same thing: we want more general audiences, to break down cultural barriers,\" he said. \"But then they come up with some very snooty(目中无人的) thing that makes you crazy.\""}, {"label": "M", "text": "John Terauds, a critic who has covered Toronto's classical music scene extensively, also wants to do away with the stuffiness. He suggested that the warmer an audience is, the better the musicians themselves will respond. \"But the producer or organizer has to let everyone know it's OK,\" he said. \"It's OK to enjoy yourself.\" At the Toronto Symphony Orchestra, for example, conductor Peter Oundjian often stops between pieces, taking a moment to talk about the composer or the music in a very amiable way. And some nights, Terauds said, \"at least a third\" of the audience consists of students who have purchased cheaper tickets. On these nights, the energy of the room drastically shifts. It becomes a less intimidating place."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Back in February, Terauds wrote on his blog about how going to classical performances can be intimidating. Certain people \"think they have to dress up,\" he wrote. \"They think they have to know something about the music before they go. And, I'm sure, sitting in a seat, trembling in fear that this might be the wrong time to applaud, is also one of the factors.\""}, {"label": "O", "text": "Everyone in the classical world agrees on the need for increased \"accessibility,\" but achieving it is often easier said than done. Nowadays, there are unknown, unorthodox opera singers wowing(博得……的喝彩) viewers on TV programs like \"America's Got Talent\" and \"The Voice\". What can higher institutions do with any of that? And if they appeal to these outlets, do they risk compromising the integrity or the intelligence of the music?"}, {"label": "P", "text": "Vulgamore seems to understand this. She thinks an organization can have it both ways, claiming the new while keeping the old. And as she reorganizes the Philadelphia Orchestra, she will attempt to do just that. \"The world's most respected musicians brought together as an orchestra will always exist,\" she said. \"But it's essential that we be willing to experiment and fail.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "How can one person enjoy good health, while another person looks old before her time? Humans have been asking this question for thousands of years, and recently, it's becoming clearer and clearer to scientists that the differences between people's rates of aging lie in the complex interactions among genes, social relationships, environments and lifestyles. Even though you were born with a particular set of genes, the way you live can influence how they express themselves. Some lifestyle factors may even turn genes on or shut them off.\nDeep within the genetic heart of all our cells are telomeres, or repeating segments of noncoding DNA that live at the ends of the chromosomes(染色体). They form caps at the ends of the chromosomes and keep the genetic material together. Shortening with each cell division, they help determine how fast a cell ages. When they become too short, the cell stops dividing altogether. This isn't the only reason a cell can age—there are other stresses on cells we don't yet understand very well—but short telomeres are one of the major reasons human cells grow old. We've devoted most of our careers to studying telomeres, and one extraordinary discovery from our labs is that telomeres can actually lengthen.\nScientists have learned that several thought patterns appear to be unhealthy for telomeres, and one of them is cynical hostility. Cynical hostility is defined by high anger and frequent thoughts that other people cannot be trusted. Someone with hostility doesn't just think, \"I hate to stand in long lines\"; they think, \"Others deliberately sped up and beat me to my rightful position in the line!\"—and then get violently agitated. People who score high on measures of cynical hostility tend to get more heart disease, metabolic disease and often die at younger ages. They also have shorter telomeres. In a study of British civil servants, men who scored high on measures of cynical hostility had shorter telomeres than men whose hostility scores were low. The most hostile men were 30% more likely to have short telomeres.\nWhat this means: aging is a dynamic process that could possibly be accelerated or slowed—and, in some aspects, even reversed. To an extent, it has surprised us and the rest of the scientific community that telomeres do not simply carry out the commands issued by your genetic code. Your telomeres are listening to you. The foods you eat, your response to challenges, the amount of exercise you get, and many other factors appear to influence your telomeres and can prevent premature aging at the cellular level. One of the keys to enjoying good health is simply doing your part to foster healthy cell renewal.", "47": "How can one person enjoy good health, while another person looks old before her time? Humans have been asking this question for thousands of years, and recently, it's becoming clearer and clearer to scientists that the differences between people's rates of aging lie in the complex interactions among genes, social relationships, environments and lifestyles. Even though you were born with a particular set of genes, the way you live can influence how they express themselves. Some lifestyle factors may even turn genes on or shut them off.\nDeep within the genetic heart of all our cells are telomeres, or repeating segments of noncoding DNA that live at the ends of the chromosomes(染色体). They form caps at the ends of the chromosomes and keep the genetic material together. Shortening with each cell division, they help determine how fast a cell ages. When they become too short, the cell stops dividing altogether. This isn't the only reason a cell can age—there are other stresses on cells we don't yet understand very well—but short telomeres are one of the major reasons human cells grow old. We've devoted most of our careers to studying telomeres, and one extraordinary discovery from our labs is that telomeres can actually lengthen.\nScientists have learned that several thought patterns appear to be unhealthy for telomeres, and one of them is cynical hostility. Cynical hostility is defined by high anger and frequent thoughts that other people cannot be trusted. Someone with hostility doesn't just think, \"I hate to stand in long lines\"; they think, \"Others deliberately sped up and beat me to my rightful position in the line!\"—and then get violently agitated. People who score high on measures of cynical hostility tend to get more heart disease, metabolic disease and often die at younger ages. They also have shorter telomeres. In a study of British civil servants, men who scored high on measures of cynical hostility had shorter telomeres than men whose hostility scores were low. The most hostile men were 30% more likely to have short telomeres.\nWhat this means: aging is a dynamic process that could possibly be accelerated or slowed—and, in some aspects, even reversed. To an extent, it has surprised us and the rest of the scientific community that telomeres do not simply carry out the commands issued by your genetic code. Your telomeres are listening to you. The foods you eat, your response to challenges, the amount of exercise you get, and many other factors appear to influence your telomeres and can prevent premature aging at the cellular level. One of the keys to enjoying good health is simply doing your part to foster healthy cell renewal.", "48": "How can one person enjoy good health, while another person looks old before her time? Humans have been asking this question for thousands of years, and recently, it's becoming clearer and clearer to scientists that the differences between people's rates of aging lie in the complex interactions among genes, social relationships, environments and lifestyles. Even though you were born with a particular set of genes, the way you live can influence how they express themselves. Some lifestyle factors may even turn genes on or shut them off.\nDeep within the genetic heart of all our cells are telomeres, or repeating segments of noncoding DNA that live at the ends of the chromosomes(染色体). They form caps at the ends of the chromosomes and keep the genetic material together. Shortening with each cell division, they help determine how fast a cell ages. When they become too short, the cell stops dividing altogether. This isn't the only reason a cell can age—there are other stresses on cells we don't yet understand very well—but short telomeres are one of the major reasons human cells grow old. We've devoted most of our careers to studying telomeres, and one extraordinary discovery from our labs is that telomeres can actually lengthen.\nScientists have learned that several thought patterns appear to be unhealthy for telomeres, and one of them is cynical hostility. Cynical hostility is defined by high anger and frequent thoughts that other people cannot be trusted. Someone with hostility doesn't just think, \"I hate to stand in long lines\"; they think, \"Others deliberately sped up and beat me to my rightful position in the line!\"—and then get violently agitated. People who score high on measures of cynical hostility tend to get more heart disease, metabolic disease and often die at younger ages. They also have shorter telomeres. In a study of British civil servants, men who scored high on measures of cynical hostility had shorter telomeres than men whose hostility scores were low. The most hostile men were 30% more likely to have short telomeres.\nWhat this means: aging is a dynamic process that could possibly be accelerated or slowed—and, in some aspects, even reversed. To an extent, it has surprised us and the rest of the scientific community that telomeres do not simply carry out the commands issued by your genetic code. Your telomeres are listening to you. The foods you eat, your response to challenges, the amount of exercise you get, and many other factors appear to influence your telomeres and can prevent premature aging at the cellular level. One of the keys to enjoying good health is simply doing your part to foster healthy cell renewal.", "49": "How can one person enjoy good health, while another person looks old before her time? Humans have been asking this question for thousands of years, and recently, it's becoming clearer and clearer to scientists that the differences between people's rates of aging lie in the complex interactions among genes, social relationships, environments and lifestyles. Even though you were born with a particular set of genes, the way you live can influence how they express themselves. Some lifestyle factors may even turn genes on or shut them off.\nDeep within the genetic heart of all our cells are telomeres, or repeating segments of noncoding DNA that live at the ends of the chromosomes(染色体). They form caps at the ends of the chromosomes and keep the genetic material together. Shortening with each cell division, they help determine how fast a cell ages. When they become too short, the cell stops dividing altogether. This isn't the only reason a cell can age—there are other stresses on cells we don't yet understand very well—but short telomeres are one of the major reasons human cells grow old. We've devoted most of our careers to studying telomeres, and one extraordinary discovery from our labs is that telomeres can actually lengthen.\nScientists have learned that several thought patterns appear to be unhealthy for telomeres, and one of them is cynical hostility. Cynical hostility is defined by high anger and frequent thoughts that other people cannot be trusted. Someone with hostility doesn't just think, \"I hate to stand in long lines\"; they think, \"Others deliberately sped up and beat me to my rightful position in the line!\"—and then get violently agitated. People who score high on measures of cynical hostility tend to get more heart disease, metabolic disease and often die at younger ages. They also have shorter telomeres. In a study of British civil servants, men who scored high on measures of cynical hostility had shorter telomeres than men whose hostility scores were low. The most hostile men were 30% more likely to have short telomeres.\nWhat this means: aging is a dynamic process that could possibly be accelerated or slowed—and, in some aspects, even reversed. To an extent, it has surprised us and the rest of the scientific community that telomeres do not simply carry out the commands issued by your genetic code. Your telomeres are listening to you. The foods you eat, your response to challenges, the amount of exercise you get, and many other factors appear to influence your telomeres and can prevent premature aging at the cellular level. One of the keys to enjoying good health is simply doing your part to foster healthy cell renewal.", "50": "How can one person enjoy good health, while another person looks old before her time? Humans have been asking this question for thousands of years, and recently, it's becoming clearer and clearer to scientists that the differences between people's rates of aging lie in the complex interactions among genes, social relationships, environments and lifestyles. Even though you were born with a particular set of genes, the way you live can influence how they express themselves. Some lifestyle factors may even turn genes on or shut them off.\nDeep within the genetic heart of all our cells are telomeres, or repeating segments of noncoding DNA that live at the ends of the chromosomes(染色体). They form caps at the ends of the chromosomes and keep the genetic material together. Shortening with each cell division, they help determine how fast a cell ages. When they become too short, the cell stops dividing altogether. This isn't the only reason a cell can age—there are other stresses on cells we don't yet understand very well—but short telomeres are one of the major reasons human cells grow old. We've devoted most of our careers to studying telomeres, and one extraordinary discovery from our labs is that telomeres can actually lengthen.\nScientists have learned that several thought patterns appear to be unhealthy for telomeres, and one of them is cynical hostility. Cynical hostility is defined by high anger and frequent thoughts that other people cannot be trusted. Someone with hostility doesn't just think, \"I hate to stand in long lines\"; they think, \"Others deliberately sped up and beat me to my rightful position in the line!\"—and then get violently agitated. People who score high on measures of cynical hostility tend to get more heart disease, metabolic disease and often die at younger ages. They also have shorter telomeres. In a study of British civil servants, men who scored high on measures of cynical hostility had shorter telomeres than men whose hostility scores were low. The most hostile men were 30% more likely to have short telomeres.\nWhat this means: aging is a dynamic process that could possibly be accelerated or slowed—and, in some aspects, even reversed. To an extent, it has surprised us and the rest of the scientific community that telomeres do not simply carry out the commands issued by your genetic code. Your telomeres are listening to you. The foods you eat, your response to challenges, the amount of exercise you get, and many other factors appear to influence your telomeres and can prevent premature aging at the cellular level. One of the keys to enjoying good health is simply doing your part to foster healthy cell renewal.", "51": "Scientists have created by accident an enzyme(酶) that breaks down plastic drinks bottles. The breakthrough could help solve the global plastic pollution crisis by enabling for the first time the full recycling of bottles.\nThe new research was spurred by the discovery in 2016 of the first bacterium that had naturally evolved to eat plastic at a waste dump in Japan. Scientists have now revealed the detailed structure of the crucial enzyme produced by the bug.\nAn international team then adjusted the enzyme to see how it had evolved, but tests showed they had accidentally made the molecule even better at breaking down the plastic used for drinks bottles. \"What actually turned out was we improved the enzyme, which was a bit of a shock,\" said head researcher Prof. McGeehan, at the University of Portsmouth, UK.\nCurrently, the enzyme takes a few days to start breaking down the plastic, far faster than the centuries it takes in the oceans, but the researchers are optimistic this can be speeded up even further and become a viable large-scale process.\n\"What we are hoping to do is use this enzyme to turn this plastic back into its original components, so we can literally recycle it back to plastic,\" said McGeehan. \"It means we won't need to dig up any more oil and, fundamentally, it should reduce the amount of plastic in the environment.\"\nAbout 1 million plastic bottles are sold each minute around the globe and, with just 14% recycled, many end up in the oceans where they have polluted even the remotest parts, harming marine life and potentially people who eat sea food. \"Plastic is incredibly resistant to degradation,\" said McGeehan. \"It is one of these wonder materials that has been made a little bit too well.\"\nCurrently those bottles that are recycled can only be turned into opaque fibres for clothing or carpets, while the new enzyme indicates a way to recycle old clear plastic bottles back into new clear plastic bottles.\n\"You are always up against the fact oil is cheap, so plastic is cheap,\" said McGeehan. \"It is so easy for manufacturers to generate more of that stuff, rather than even try to recycle, but I believe there is a public interest here: perception is changing so much that companies are starting to look at how they can properly recycle these bottles.\"\nProf. Adisa Azapagic, at the University of Manchester in the UK, agreed the enzyme could be useful but added: \"A full life-cycle assessment would be needed to ensure that the technology does not solve one environmental problem—waste—at the expense of others, including additional greenhouse gas emissions.\"", "52": "Scientists have created by accident an enzyme(酶) that breaks down plastic drinks bottles. The breakthrough could help solve the global plastic pollution crisis by enabling for the first time the full recycling of bottles.\nThe new research was spurred by the discovery in 2016 of the first bacterium that had naturally evolved to eat plastic at a waste dump in Japan. Scientists have now revealed the detailed structure of the crucial enzyme produced by the bug.\nAn international team then adjusted the enzyme to see how it had evolved, but tests showed they had accidentally made the molecule even better at breaking down the plastic used for drinks bottles. \"What actually turned out was we improved the enzyme, which was a bit of a shock,\" said head researcher Prof. McGeehan, at the University of Portsmouth, UK.\nCurrently, the enzyme takes a few days to start breaking down the plastic, far faster than the centuries it takes in the oceans, but the researchers are optimistic this can be speeded up even further and become a viable large-scale process.\n\"What we are hoping to do is use this enzyme to turn this plastic back into its original components, so we can literally recycle it back to plastic,\" said McGeehan. \"It means we won't need to dig up any more oil and, fundamentally, it should reduce the amount of plastic in the environment.\"\nAbout 1 million plastic bottles are sold each minute around the globe and, with just 14% recycled, many end up in the oceans where they have polluted even the remotest parts, harming marine life and potentially people who eat sea food. \"Plastic is incredibly resistant to degradation,\" said McGeehan. \"It is one of these wonder materials that has been made a little bit too well.\"\nCurrently those bottles that are recycled can only be turned into opaque fibres for clothing or carpets, while the new enzyme indicates a way to recycle old clear plastic bottles back into new clear plastic bottles.\n\"You are always up against the fact oil is cheap, so plastic is cheap,\" said McGeehan. \"It is so easy for manufacturers to generate more of that stuff, rather than even try to recycle, but I believe there is a public interest here: perception is changing so much that companies are starting to look at how they can properly recycle these bottles.\"\nProf. Adisa Azapagic, at the University of Manchester in the UK, agreed the enzyme could be useful but added: \"A full life-cycle assessment would be needed to ensure that the technology does not solve one environmental problem—waste—at the expense of others, including additional greenhouse gas emissions.\"", "53": "Scientists have created by accident an enzyme(酶) that breaks down plastic drinks bottles. The breakthrough could help solve the global plastic pollution crisis by enabling for the first time the full recycling of bottles.\nThe new research was spurred by the discovery in 2016 of the first bacterium that had naturally evolved to eat plastic at a waste dump in Japan. Scientists have now revealed the detailed structure of the crucial enzyme produced by the bug.\nAn international team then adjusted the enzyme to see how it had evolved, but tests showed they had accidentally made the molecule even better at breaking down the plastic used for drinks bottles. \"What actually turned out was we improved the enzyme, which was a bit of a shock,\" said head researcher Prof. McGeehan, at the University of Portsmouth, UK.\nCurrently, the enzyme takes a few days to start breaking down the plastic, far faster than the centuries it takes in the oceans, but the researchers are optimistic this can be speeded up even further and become a viable large-scale process.\n\"What we are hoping to do is use this enzyme to turn this plastic back into its original components, so we can literally recycle it back to plastic,\" said McGeehan. \"It means we won't need to dig up any more oil and, fundamentally, it should reduce the amount of plastic in the environment.\"\nAbout 1 million plastic bottles are sold each minute around the globe and, with just 14% recycled, many end up in the oceans where they have polluted even the remotest parts, harming marine life and potentially people who eat sea food. \"Plastic is incredibly resistant to degradation,\" said McGeehan. \"It is one of these wonder materials that has been made a little bit too well.\"\nCurrently those bottles that are recycled can only be turned into opaque fibres for clothing or carpets, while the new enzyme indicates a way to recycle old clear plastic bottles back into new clear plastic bottles.\n\"You are always up against the fact oil is cheap, so plastic is cheap,\" said McGeehan. \"It is so easy for manufacturers to generate more of that stuff, rather than even try to recycle, but I believe there is a public interest here: perception is changing so much that companies are starting to look at how they can properly recycle these bottles.\"\nProf. Adisa Azapagic, at the University of Manchester in the UK, agreed the enzyme could be useful but added: \"A full life-cycle assessment would be needed to ensure that the technology does not solve one environmental problem—waste—at the expense of others, including additional greenhouse gas emissions.\"", "54": "Scientists have created by accident an enzyme(酶) that breaks down plastic drinks bottles. The breakthrough could help solve the global plastic pollution crisis by enabling for the first time the full recycling of bottles.\nThe new research was spurred by the discovery in 2016 of the first bacterium that had naturally evolved to eat plastic at a waste dump in Japan. Scientists have now revealed the detailed structure of the crucial enzyme produced by the bug.\nAn international team then adjusted the enzyme to see how it had evolved, but tests showed they had accidentally made the molecule even better at breaking down the plastic used for drinks bottles. \"What actually turned out was we improved the enzyme, which was a bit of a shock,\" said head researcher Prof. McGeehan, at the University of Portsmouth, UK.\nCurrently, the enzyme takes a few days to start breaking down the plastic, far faster than the centuries it takes in the oceans, but the researchers are optimistic this can be speeded up even further and become a viable large-scale process.\n\"What we are hoping to do is use this enzyme to turn this plastic back into its original components, so we can literally recycle it back to plastic,\" said McGeehan. \"It means we won't need to dig up any more oil and, fundamentally, it should reduce the amount of plastic in the environment.\"\nAbout 1 million plastic bottles are sold each minute around the globe and, with just 14% recycled, many end up in the oceans where they have polluted even the remotest parts, harming marine life and potentially people who eat sea food. \"Plastic is incredibly resistant to degradation,\" said McGeehan. \"It is one of these wonder materials that has been made a little bit too well.\"\nCurrently those bottles that are recycled can only be turned into opaque fibres for clothing or carpets, while the new enzyme indicates a way to recycle old clear plastic bottles back into new clear plastic bottles.\n\"You are always up against the fact oil is cheap, so plastic is cheap,\" said McGeehan. \"It is so easy for manufacturers to generate more of that stuff, rather than even try to recycle, but I believe there is a public interest here: perception is changing so much that companies are starting to look at how they can properly recycle these bottles.\"\nProf. Adisa Azapagic, at the University of Manchester in the UK, agreed the enzyme could be useful but added: \"A full life-cycle assessment would be needed to ensure that the technology does not solve one environmental problem—waste—at the expense of others, including additional greenhouse gas emissions.\"", "55": "Scientists have created by accident an enzyme(酶) that breaks down plastic drinks bottles. The breakthrough could help solve the global plastic pollution crisis by enabling for the first time the full recycling of bottles.\nThe new research was spurred by the discovery in 2016 of the first bacterium that had naturally evolved to eat plastic at a waste dump in Japan. Scientists have now revealed the detailed structure of the crucial enzyme produced by the bug.\nAn international team then adjusted the enzyme to see how it had evolved, but tests showed they had accidentally made the molecule even better at breaking down the plastic used for drinks bottles. \"What actually turned out was we improved the enzyme, which was a bit of a shock,\" said head researcher Prof. McGeehan, at the University of Portsmouth, UK.\nCurrently, the enzyme takes a few days to start breaking down the plastic, far faster than the centuries it takes in the oceans, but the researchers are optimistic this can be speeded up even further and become a viable large-scale process.\n\"What we are hoping to do is use this enzyme to turn this plastic back into its original components, so we can literally recycle it back to plastic,\" said McGeehan. \"It means we won't need to dig up any more oil and, fundamentally, it should reduce the amount of plastic in the environment.\"\nAbout 1 million plastic bottles are sold each minute around the globe and, with just 14% recycled, many end up in the oceans where they have polluted even the remotest parts, harming marine life and potentially people who eat sea food. \"Plastic is incredibly resistant to degradation,\" said McGeehan. \"It is one of these wonder materials that has been made a little bit too well.\"\nCurrently those bottles that are recycled can only be turned into opaque fibres for clothing or carpets, while the new enzyme indicates a way to recycle old clear plastic bottles back into new clear plastic bottles.\n\"You are always up against the fact oil is cheap, so plastic is cheap,\" said McGeehan. \"It is so easy for manufacturers to generate more of that stuff, rather than even try to recycle, but I believe there is a public interest here: perception is changing so much that companies are starting to look at how they can properly recycle these bottles.\"\nProf. Adisa Azapagic, at the University of Manchester in the UK, agreed the enzyme could be useful but added: \"A full life-cycle assessment would be needed to ensure that the technology does not solve one environmental problem—waste—at the expense of others, including additional greenhouse gas emissions.\""}, "questions": [{"id": "2022-12-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abandoned", "B) access", "C) consumption", "D) contrive", "E) duplications", "F) investments", "G) mobilized", "H) negligent", "I) neutral", "J) reductions", "K) released", "L) replacement", "M) slash", "N) surplus", "O) void"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) released\n动词\n【词性槽】「a report ___ yesterday」report 后修饰、yesterday 锁过去时 → 过去分词作后置定语\n【依据】release a report = 发布报告，是固定搭配\nsaid in a report __(26)__ yesterday 中 release a report（发布报告）是新闻语篇里的典型\n搭配，与「EIA 昨天发布的一份报告里说……」语义完全咬合。\n【竞争词】G) mobilized mobilize a report 不通\nmobilize 与「人力、资源、军队」搭，从不修饰 report；语义上「动员一份报告」也讲不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abandoned", "B) access", "C) consumption", "D) contrive", "E) duplications", "F) investments", "G) mobilized", "H) negligent", "I) neutral", "J) reductions", "K) released", "L) replacement", "M) slash", "N) surplus", "O) void"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) abandoned\n动词\n【词性槽】「have ___ coal completely」现在完成时缺过去分词\n【依据】「彻底放弃燃煤」与全文「减煤」主线完全吻合\n20 have __(27)__ coal completely 与 American colleges and universities are using 64\npercent less coal than they did a decade ago 形成程度递进——大多数学校少用，20 所\n「彻底放弃」。abandon coal 是脱煤议题的高频表达。\n【竞争词】G) mobilized 「动员了煤炭」与「停用煤炭」方向相反\nmobilize 意为「动员、调动」，have mobilized coal completely 等于「已经把煤完全动员起\n来用」，与「彻底放弃」截然相反，逻辑错位。\nK) released release coal 不通且与上下文矛盾\nrelease coal 不是常用搭配（release 多与 report、information、emissions 搭），且与「使\n用」无关；后续 26 题更需要 released 来搭配 report，此处不应抢用。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 K) —— released 词性形态都对，最易抢走 27 题。have ___ coal\ncompletely 处 K)released 形态完全合规，且 release 在新闻语体里高频；但 release coal\n不是自然搭配，且 26 题 a report ___ yesterday 才是 released 的归属。考生若不按「先易\n后难、用过即划」的顺序，容易把 K 提前用到 27 题、导致 26 题再也找不到合适的过去分\n词。防坑：先做 26 题（因为 a report released yesterday 的搭配更硬），把 K 划走，27\n题就自然落到 abandoned 上。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abandoned", "B) access", "C) consumption", "D) contrive", "E) duplications", "F) investments", "G) mobilized", "H) negligent", "I) neutral", "J) reductions", "K) released", "L) replacement", "M) slash", "N) surplus", "O) void"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) replacement\n名词\n【词性槽】不定冠词 a 后的空 → 单数名词\n【依据】「转向天然气作为替代品」语义最贴\nMost universities have turned to natural gas as a __(28)__, with state funding\nbacking the fuel switch. 句末 the fuel switch（这次燃料切换）直接点题——天然气是\n「替代品」，replacement 与 fuel switch 形成同义复现。\n【竞争词】C) consumption 义指消耗量，与「替代」无关\nconsumption 表「消耗、消费」，turn to natural gas as a consumption 不通——天然气不\n是「一种消耗」，而是「煤的替代」。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abandoned", "B) access", "C) consumption", "D) contrive", "E) duplications", "F) investments", "G) mobilized", "H) negligent", "I) neutral", "J) reductions", "K) released", "L) replacement", "M) slash", "N) surplus", "O) void"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) access\n名词\n【词性槽】「__ to power was scarce」做主语，缺主语名词\n【依据】access to power = 用电的途径\ncampus coal use has a history dating back to the 1800s when __(29)__ to power was\nscarce 1800 年代「用电途径稀缺」，故学校自建煤电厂——语义和逻辑都通；access to\n是六级听读写都常考的固定搭配。\n【竞争词】C) consumption consumption of/in，不与 to 搭配\nconsumption 后通常接 of，「consumption to power」不是英语习惯表达；语义上「电力消\n费稀缺」也讲不通，因为后文说要自建电厂正是为了「获取电」而非「消费电」。\nF) investments 复数主语需 were，且 investments to power 不通\ninvestments 是复数，与 was 不一致；且 investments in 才是常见搭配，「投资稀缺」也偏离\n「为何要自建煤电厂」的因果。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abandoned", "B) access", "C) consumption", "D) contrive", "E) duplications", "F) investments", "G) mobilized", "H) negligent", "I) neutral", "J) reductions", "K) released", "L) replacement", "M) slash", "N) surplus", "O) void"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) surplus\n形容词\n【词性槽】「sell ___ power to utilities」名词 power 前需形容词修饰\n【依据】surplus power = 剩余电力，正是发电后多余卖给电网的部分\nMany universities still operate their own power plants. The Public Utility Regulatory\nPolicies Act of 1978 encouraged more electricity generation by allowing institutions\nto sell __(30)__ power to utilities. 大学自办电厂、鼓励多发电——多发出来的电只能是\n「剩余的」，卖给公共电网（utilities），surplus 完美对应。\n【竞争词】O) void void power 语义错位\nvoid 表「无效的、空的」，void power 字面意思偏向「无效电力 / 空的功率」，与「多发的电\n卖给电网」完全相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abandoned", "B) access", "C) consumption", "D) contrive", "E) duplications", "F) investments", "G) mobilized", "H) negligent", "I) neutral", "J) reductions", "K) released", "L) replacement", "M) slash", "N) surplus", "O) void"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) slash\n动词\n【词性槽】「aims to ___ greenhouse gas emissions」不定式 to 后接动词原形\n【依据】slash emissions = 大幅削减排放，新闻语篇高频搭配\naims to __(31)__ greenhouse gas emissions 与下一句 pledged to be carbon __(32)__\nwithin 20 years 形成「大幅削减 → 实现碳中和」的目标链；slash 表「大幅削减」，正是\n减排议题中比 reduce 更有力的词。\n【竞争词】D) contrive contrive 多接 to do，不直接带 emissions\ncontrive 表「谋划、设法做成」，常用结构是 contrive to do something，不接 emissions 这\n类名词宾语；语义上也偏「图谋、设计」，与减排目标不搭。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 D) —— contrive 是动词原形、看似能填 to ___ 位。aims to\n__(31)__ greenhouse gas emissions 处 contrive 形态正合「to + 动词原形」，且词面冷\n僻、容易让考生「越冷越对」地误选；但 contrive 不能直接带 emissions 作宾语，语义也\n偏「图谋」。防坑：减排议题里 to + 动词 + emissions 几乎只配 cut/slash/reduce/curb\n这一类「强削减动词」，背主题搭配最稳。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abandoned", "B) access", "C) consumption", "D) contrive", "E) duplications", "F) investments", "G) mobilized", "H) negligent", "I) neutral", "J) reductions", "K) released", "L) replacement", "M) slash", "N) surplus", "O) void"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) neutral\n形容词\n【词性槽】「to be carbon ___ within 20 years」处在不定式 be 的表语位置\n【依据】carbon neutral = 碳中和，正是减排承诺的目标\nAbout 665 schools are part of the program, which aims to __(31)__ greenhouse gas\nemissions. Thirty percent of the participants have pledged to be carbon __(32)__\nwithin 20 years. 上一句讲削减温室气体排放，下一句承诺「20 年内 carbon ___」——只\n有 neutral 能与 carbon 组成减排议题的核心术语，对应「净零排放」。\n【竞争词】H) negligent 义为「玩忽职守」，与碳排话题不搭\nnegligent 指人疏忽、失职，从不与 carbon 搭配，语义方向也与「减排承诺」相反。\nN) surplus 「碳过剩」不是承诺目标\ncarbon surplus 一来不是固定术语，二来「碳过剩」是要避免的问题，而非「pledged to be」\n的对象。\nO) void 「carbon void」非术语、且 void 多与 of 连用\nvoid 作形容词常说 void of/null and void，与 carbon 不构成减排领域的固定搭配，意思也与\n「碳中和」对不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abandoned", "B) access", "C) consumption", "D) contrive", "E) duplications", "F) investments", "G) mobilized", "H) negligent", "I) neutral", "J) reductions", "K) released", "L) replacement", "M) slash", "N) surplus", "O) void"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) investments\n名词\n【词性槽】「withdraw their ___ in coal」中物主代词 their 后 + 介词 in，必为名词\n【依据】withdraw investments in fossil fuels = 撤资行动\ncampaigns for universities to withdraw their __(33)__ in coal and other fossil fuels 与\nBeyond Coal 运动一致，呼吁大学从化石燃料中「撤资」——withdraw investments in\n是经济议题的固定搭配，复数 investments 也与 their 自然搭配。\n【竞争词】C) consumption 「撤回消费」语义怪\nwithdraw their consumption in coal 不是英语习惯说法；且后文 22 所高校宣布「move\nbeyond coal」是从资本层面撤离，不只是减少使用。\nJ) reductions 「撤回削减」逻辑反了\nreductions 指「削减」，withdraw their reductions 等于「撤回削减」，与全文主旨完全相反\n——campaigns 的目的不是阻止削减，而是推动撤资以加速脱煤。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abandoned", "B) access", "C) consumption", "D) contrive", "E) duplications", "F) investments", "G) mobilized", "H) negligent", "I) neutral", "J) reductions", "K) released", "L) replacement", "M) slash", "N) surplus", "O) void"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) reductions\n名词\n【词性槽】「The largest coal use ___ at colleges were in Michigan...」主语缺名词中心，谓语\nwere 提示复数\n【依据】the largest coal use reductions = 最大的煤炭使用削减量\nThe largest coal use __(34)__ at colleges were in Michigan, Missouri, Tennessee and\nIndiana. 与下文 Indiana's universities alone cut coal __(35)__ by 81 percent 同义复现\n——「削减幅度最大的州」与「印第安纳州削减 81%」对应，reductions 与 cut 是同根名\n动呼应。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abandoned", "B) access", "C) consumption", "D) contrive", "E) duplications", "F) investments", "G) mobilized", "H) negligent", "I) neutral", "J) reductions", "K) released", "L) replacement", "M) slash", "N) surplus", "O) void"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) consumption\n名词\n【词性槽】「cut coal ___ by 81 percent」动词 cut 后名词 coal 再 + 空 → 名词作 cut 的宾语\n【依据】coal consumption = 煤炭消耗量，可被 81% 量化\nIndiana's universities alone cut coal __(35)__ by 81 percent between 2008 and 2015.\n与 American colleges and universities are using 64 percent less coal 形成同义复现\n——coal consumption 是 using…coal 的名词化表达，与 by 81 percent 完美咬合。\n【竞争词】E) duplications duplications 与 coal 无搭配关系\nduplications 表「复制品、重复」，从不与 coal 搭配，也无法被「by 百分比」量化为能源指\n标；语义完全偏离主题。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "It was not a rare occurrence that audiences behaved wildly while listening to classical music.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】观众行为狂野并不罕见\n【定位】The truth is that classical music audiences weren't always so polite. Robert Greenberg, an\naward-winning composer, said that when Beethoven first performed his 7th Symphony,\naudiences forced the orchestra to perform encores(重演) of certain movements immediately,\napplauding wildly. And in the last few decades, he said, many audiences at opera\nperformances have abandoned pretenses, yelling \"Bravo\" when they feel like it.\n事实是古典音乐的观众并非一直这么有礼貌。获奖作曲家罗伯特·格林伯格说，贝多芬首演第\n七交响曲时，观众逼着乐团当场重演某些乐章、狂热鼓掌；他还说近几十年里许多歌剧观众\n也撕下了正经的面子，想喊就喊 Bravo。\n【改写】It was not a rare occurrence that audiences behaved wildly while listening to classical music. ↔\naudiences forced the orchestra to perform encores(重演) of certain movements immediately,\napplauding wildly\n否定双重转正后同义重合\n【辨邻】 A In 1913, classical music sparked a riot in Paris 只是孤例，而题干要求 not a rare\noccurrence——多次、常见，A段无法支撑这一概括。\nIn 1913, classical music sparked a riot in Paris", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Some high-art institutions don't actually mean it when they say they want more general audiences.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】高雅机构口是心非要更广观众\n【定位】\"On one hand, these organizations are all saying the same thing: we want more general\naudiences, to break down cultural barriers,\" he said. \"But then they come up with some very\nsnooty(目中无人的) thing that makes you crazy.\"\n他说：一方面，这些机构嘴上都讲同一套话——我们想要更多大众观众、想打破文化壁垒；\n另一方面，他们又会做出一些非常目中无人的事，让你抓狂。\n【改写】Some high-art institutions don't actually mean it when they say they want more general audiences.\n↔\nthese organizations are all saying the same thing: we want more general audiences, to break\ndown cultural barriers\n口号与反向行动并置即言行不一\n【辨邻】 H it's about opening up \"access.\" 是正面陈述如何打开通道，没有 don't actually\nmean it 那种揭穿言行不一的转折结构。\nRather, it's about opening up \"access.\"", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "The theatre was in chaos when an unconventional ballet was first put on stage in the capital of France.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】法国首都首演非常规芭蕾引混乱\n【定位】In 1913, classical music sparked a riot in Paris. Igor Stravinsky was introducing his\nrevolutionary \"Rite of Spring\" ballet to the world, with its discordant melodies and\nunorthodox choreography(编舞), and the purists in the crowd expressed their disapproval\nloud and clear.\n1913年，古典音乐在巴黎引发了一场骚乱。伊戈尔·斯特拉文斯基正在向世界推出他革命性\n的《春之祭》芭蕾舞剧，其中有不和谐的旋律与非常规的编舞，人群里的纯粹派把他们的不\n满表达得清清楚楚、震天响地。\n【改写】in the capital of France ↔ in Paris\n上义短语指代专名\nan unconventional ballet ↔\nhis revolutionary \"Rite of Spring\" ballet to the world, with its discordant melodies and\nunorthodox choreography(编舞)\n同义形容词替换\nThe theatre was in chaos ↔ The noise, fighting, and shouting in the audience got so loud\n上位名词概括场面", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "According to one critic, the audience's warm response would encourage the musicians to do a better job.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】评论家说观众热情回应能激励乐手\n【定位】He suggested that the warmer an audience is, the better the musicians themselves\nwill respond.\n他指出，观众越热情，乐手自己就会回应得越好。\n【改写】the audience's warm response would encourage the musicians to do a better job ↔\nthe warmer an audience is, the better the musicians themselves will respond\n越…越… 即正向因果\nAccording to one critic ↔\nJohn Terauds, a critic who has covered Toronto's classical music scene extensively\n身份直接对应\n【辨邻】 F Greenberg 在文中是 Robert Greenberg, an award-winning composer 的作曲家身\n份，与题干 one critic 不符。\nI don't think there's anything wrong with an audience showing their enthusiasm for a proper\nmoment by applauding, showing their joy", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Many commenters argued for the audience enjoying classical music quietly.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】众多评论者主张安静聆听\n【定位】The Huffington Post recently ran a Great Debate on this issue and many commenters came\nout on the side of silence. \"There is no more rewarding experience in life than being part of\nan audience where everybody is leaning forward in silence, thoroughly carried away by a\ngreat performance of a masterpiece,\" one commenter wrote.\n《赫芬顿邮报》最近就这一问题发起了一场大讨论，许多留言者站在沉默一边。一位留言者\n写道：人生最有回报的体验，莫过于成为一群观众中的一员——每个人都向前倾着身体、屏\n息凝神，被一首杰作的伟大演奏彻底带走。\n【改写】Many commenters argued for the audience enjoying classical music quietly. ↔\nmany commenters came out on the side of silence\nargue for 与 came out on the side of 同义\n【辨邻】 B many of the genre's biggest supporters would have it no other way 描述的是古\n典乐拥护者一般态度，并非 Many commenters 这种公开发表评论的人。\nmany of the genre's biggest supporters would have it no other way", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "What appears on the seatback screen makes it unnecessary for the audience to study the works beforehand.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】椅背屏幕字幕免去事先研习\n【定位】When operas first instituted subtitles(字幕) during shows, he said, many purists didn't like the\nidea, believing that the audience should instead study the works before attending. But now it's\ncommonplace to find titles on the seatback in front of you—choose a language, sit back, and\nunderstand what's going on.\n他说，当歌剧最初在演出中引入字幕时，许多纯粹派并不喜欢这个主意，他们认为观众应当\n事先研习作品；但如今前排椅背上配有字幕已经是寻常事——选一种语言、坐回去、就能看\n懂整场。\n【改写】What appears on the seatback screen ↔ find titles on the seatback in front of you\n同义短语指代字幕\nmakes it unnecessary for the audience to study the works beforehand ↔\nmany purists didn't like the idea, believing that the audience should instead study the works\nbefore attending\n对比转折显示已不必要\n【辨邻】 I where people like to learn 是泛泛而谈互动学习，并未与 seatback screen 字幕相\n关联。\nWe are an interactive society now, where people like to learn", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "It is generally accepted that there should be no disturbance from the audience during classical music performance.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】普遍接受演出时观众不应打扰\n【定位】It's difficult to imagine a similar disturbance occurring today within America's sacred\nsymphony halls. In fact, it's hard to picture any kind of disruptive activity at all (unless\nsomeone's cell phone happens to go off, and then you'd better watch your back). A mannerly\naura(氛围) hangs over most classical proceedings, and many of the genre's biggest supporters\nwould have it no other way.\n很难想象今天美国神圣的交响乐厅里会发生类似的骚动，事实上几乎任何形式的扰乱都难以\n想象（除非有人手机响起来，那你就要小心后果了）。一种讲究礼仪的氛围弥漫在大多数古\n典音乐演出中，这一行当最大的拥护者们也乐于让它保持原样。\n【改写】there should be no disturbance from the audience during classical music performance ↔\nit's hard to picture any kind of disruptive activity at all\n同根同义否定\nIt is generally accepted ↔ many of the genre's biggest supporters would have it no other way\n全称式认同等价普遍接受\n【辨邻】 D many commenters came out on the side of silence 只是评论区里一派意见，并非\ngenerally accepted 所要求的全场共识。\nmany commenters came out on the side of silence", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Higher institutions will be concerned about compromising the integrity of classical music if they have to resort to the television medium.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】借助电视渠道可能折损音乐完整性\n【定位】Nowadays, there are unknown, unorthodox opera singers wowing(博得……的喝彩) viewers\non TV programs like \"America's Got Talent\" and \"The Voice\". What can higher institutions\ndo with any of that? And if they appeal to these outlets, do they risk compromising the\nintegrity or the intelligence of the music?\n如今电视节目如《美国达人秀》和《好声音》里，有些名不见经传的非正统歌剧选手赢得了\n观众喝彩。高雅机构能拿这些做什么？而如果它们去借助这些渠道，会不会冒着损害音乐的\n完整性或智识水准的风险？\n【改写】Higher institutions will be concerned about compromising the integrity of classical music ↔\ndo they risk compromising the integrity or the intelligence of the music\n反问表担忧等价陈述\nif they have to resort to the television medium ↔ if they appeal to these outlets\noutlets 指代 TV programs\n【辨邻】 C how the medium can weave itself into the 21st century's cultural fabric without\nsacrificing its integrity 没有涉及 television medium 这一具体渠道。\nhow the medium can weave itself into the 21st century's cultural fabric without sacrificing\nits integrity", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Heavily discounted rush tickets help attract many young students to attend classical concerts.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】大幅打折的尾票吸引学生\n【定位】The orchestra also continues to offer $25 annual memberships to Philadelphia students, who\ncan buy rush tickets to every concert on the schedule. \"Students line up for the concerts they\nwant, and we get roughly 300 or 350 kids a night coming to these. They take any of the open\nseats available, 5 minutes before the concert starts,\" Vulgamore said.\n乐团还持续向费城的学生提供25美元的年度会籍，凭这个会籍他们可以购买每场演出的尾\n票。瓦尔加莫尔说：学生们为他们想看的音乐会排队，我们每晚大约迎来三百到三百五十名\n孩子。他们在开场前五分钟拿走任何还空着的座位。\n【改写】Heavily discounted rush tickets ↔\n$25 annual memberships to Philadelphia students, who can buy rush tickets to every concert\non the schedule\n数字 + 尾票即重折扣\nattract many young students to attend classical concerts ↔\nwe get roughly 300 or 350 kids a night coming to these\n三百多名孩子每晚到场即大量吸引\n【辨邻】 G more likely to head to Central Park to watch a free performance with a bottle of\nwine and their friends 说的是免费户外演出，与 Heavily discounted rush tickets\n指向不同渠道。\nThey're probably more likely to head to Central Park to watch a free performance with a bottle of\nwine and their friends", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "The formalities of high-art theatres can intimidate some people attending a performance.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】高雅剧院的规矩让人望而生畏\n【定位】Back in February, Terauds wrote on his blog about how going to classical performances can\nbe intimidating. Certain people \"think they have to dress up,\" he wrote. \"They think they have\nto know something about the music before they go. And, I'm sure, sitting in a seat, trembling\nin fear that this might be the wrong time to applaud, is also one of the factors.\"\n二月时，特劳兹在博客里写过，去古典音乐演出会让人望而生畏。他写道：某些人觉得他们\n必须盛装出席。他们觉得必须事先了解音乐。我也相信，坐在座位上、生怕这一刻鼓掌是错\n的而瑟瑟发抖，也是原因之一。\n【改写】The formalities of high-art theatres ↔ think they have to dress up\n上位名词概括形式规矩\ncan intimidate some people attending a performance ↔\ngoing to classical performances can be intimidating\n同根同义\n【辨邻】 C Western audiences for classical music and opera and ballet are almost always\nwell dressed, older, respectful, achingly silent and often very wealthy 只是事实画\n像，并未表达 intimidate 这一情绪结论。\nWestern audiences for classical music and opera and ballet are almost always well dressed, older,\nrespectful, achingly silent and often very wealthy", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What have scientists come to know better today?", "options": ["A) Why people age at different rates.", "B) How genes influence the aging process.", "C) How various genes express themselves in aging.", "D) Why people have long been concerned about aging."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What have scientists come to know better today?问的是科学家\"已经\"了解到的客观事\n实，要求回原文找事实句，不需要推断。\n【拆句】结构平铺直叙：主语scientists + 谓语have come to know（\"逐渐了解到\"，动词短语作完\n成时）+ 比较级状语better today。无陷阱，唯一要注意的是come to know是\"逐渐认识\n到\"，非\"来认识\"。\n【定位】Humans have been asking this question for thousands of years, and recently, it's becoming\nclearer and clearer to scientists that the differences between people's rates of aging lie in the\ncomplex interactions among genes, social relationships, environments and lifestyles.\n几千年来人类一直在问这个问题，而最近，科学家越来越清楚地认识到：人与人之间衰老速\n度的差异，源于基因、社会关系、环境和生活方式之间的复杂互动。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe differences between people's rates of aging ↔ Why people age at different rates\nit's becoming clearer and clearer to scientists ↔ come to know better\n预判\"为何人们衰老速度不同\"与Why people age at different rates逐词同义对应，且A\n完整覆盖了定位句的核心信息（差异+原因）。\nB) 过度推断\n定位句只说\"差异源于基因等多因素互动\"，并未具体阐述\"基因如何影响衰老过程\"；选项\nHow genes influence the aging process问的是机制原理，是原文没展开的内容。\nC) 无中生有\n定位句只点名\"基因等因素互动\"，原文Even though you were born with a particular\nset of genes, the way you live can influence how they express themselves.虽提到\n基因表达，但并未说\"各种基因如何在衰老中表达自己\"——选项C说的various genes在\n衰老中\"各自如何表达\"原文未有此层级讨论。\nD) 答非所问\nWhy people have long been concerned about aging问的是\"人们为何长期关注衰\n老\"，对应原文Humans have been asking this question for thousands of years提到\n的\"已问数千年\"——但这是背景，不是\"科学家最近搞清楚的事\"，与题干come to know\nbetter today错位。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "Why are some lifestyle factors considered extremely important?", "options": ["A) They may shorten the process of cell division.", "B) They may determine how genes function.", "C) They may affect the lifespan of telomeres.", "D) They may account for the stresses on cells."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Why are some lifestyle factors considered extremely important?问的是被认定为重要\n的客观理由，在原文有对应解释句。\n【拆句】被动语态主干lifestyle factors are considered extremely important，相当于\"people\nconsider lifestyle factors extremely important\"。注意被动里considered是\"被认为\"，不\n是\"考虑\"，读出主动意思即可。\n【定位】Even though you were born with a particular set of genes, the way you live can influence how\nthey express themselves. Some lifestyle factors may even turn genes on or shut them off.\n尽管你生来就带着一套特定的基因，但你的生活方式可以影响这些基因如何表达。某些生活\n方式因素甚至能把基因\"打开\"或\"关闭\"。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文从未提及生活方式与\"细胞分裂过程的缩短\"有关；shorten the process of cell\ndivision中的\"cell division\"虽在P2出现，但与lifestyle factors并无原文连接。\nB) ✓ 正确\nturn genes on or shut them off ↔ determine how genes function\nthe way you live can influence how they express themselves ↔\ndetermine how genes function\n预判\"能决定基因的工作方式\"与determine how genes function逐词对应；且原文\"开\n关基因\"这一极端动作正是\"决定基因如何工作\"的最强佐证。\nC) 张冠李戴\naffect the lifespan of telomeres说\"影响端粒寿命\"，但定位句在P1，此处尚未引入\ntelomeres概念；telomeres是P2才出现的话题，与第47题对接的lifestyle factors考点\n句不在同一区间。\nD) 与原文相反\naccount for the stresses on cells说生活方式\"解释了细胞所受的压力\"，但原文there\nare other stresses on cells we don't yet understand very well明确说细胞的其他压\n力\"我们还不太了解\"——既然还没搞清楚，就谈不上由生活方式\"解释\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What have the author and his colleagues discovered about telomeres?", "options": ["A) Their number affects the growth of cells.", "B) Their length determines the quality of life.", "C) Their shortening process can be reversed.", "D) Their health impacts the division of cells."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What have the author and his colleagues discovered about telomeres?问的是已经\"发\n现\"了的客观结论，原文必有对应陈述。\n【拆句】现在完成时疑问句，主干the author and his colleagues have discovered后接介词短语\nabout telomeres限定话题。结构无陷阱，重点是发现的内容（discovered的宾语，需到原\n文找）。\n【定位】We've devoted most of our careers to studying telomeres, and one extraordinary discovery\nfrom our labs is that telomeres can actually lengthen.\n我们把职业生涯的大部分时间都投入到对端粒的研究中，而我们实验室一个非凡的发现是：\n端粒其实可以延长。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文从未提及\"端粒的\"数量\"（number）影响细胞生长\"；端粒只谈\"长短\"，未\n谈\"多少\"。\nB) 过度推断\n原文谈端粒影响\"细胞衰老速度\"和\"人类细胞何时停止分裂\"，但从未上升到the quality\nof life（生活质量）这一宏观概念——\"生活质量\"是远超细胞机制的延伸推断。\nC) ✓ 正确\ntelomeres can actually lengthen ↔ Their shortening process can be reversed\none extraordinary discovery from our labs ↔ the author and his colleagues discovered\n预判\"端粒缩短的趋势可被逆转\"与Their shortening process can be reversed核心动词\nreverse直接对应；且\"端粒能变长\"在本段\"只会变短\"的基线之上，唯一合理解读就是\"缩\n短可被逆转\"。\nD) 无中生有\n原文是\"端粒变短到一定程度，细胞就停止分裂\"——细胞分裂决定于端粒长度，而非端\n粒\"健康\"影响细胞分裂。选项Their health impacts the division of cells把端粒\"健康状\n态\"当原因，且\"健康\"本身原文未提及。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What have scientists learned about cynical hostility?", "options": ["A) It may lead to confrontational thought patterns.", "B) It may produce an adverse effect on telomeres.", "C) It may cause people to lose their temper frequently.", "D) It may stir up agitation among those in long lines."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What have scientists learned about cynical hostility?问的是科学家研究后得出的客观结\n论，归细节题。\n【拆句】现在完成时疑问句，结构与第48题同型。注意cynical意为\"愤世嫉俗的、玩世不恭的\"，\nhostility是\"敌意\"，合起来指一种带不信任色彩的敌意性格。\n【定位】Scientists have learned that several thought patterns appear to be unhealthy for telomeres, and\none of them is cynical hostility.\n科学家发现，有几种思维模式似乎对端粒有害，其中之一就是\"愤世嫉俗式敌意\"。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nlead to confrontational thought patterns说cynical hostility\"导致对抗性思维模\n式\"——但cynical hostility本身就是一种thought pattern（思维模式），不存在\"导致\n另一思维模式\"的链条；题干问的是其后果，A把因果维度错位。\nB) ✓ 正确\nunhealthy for telomeres ↔ produce an adverse effect on telomeres\nThey also have shorter telomeres. ↔ produce an adverse effect on telomeres\n预判\"对端粒有害\"与produce an adverse effect on telomeres词义直接对应；且后文\nhad shorter telomeres的实证数据更强化了这一判断。\nC) 因果颠倒\ncause people to lose their temper frequently说敌意\"导致人们频繁发脾气\"，但原文\n里\"经常的愤怒\"(frequent anger)是cynical hostility的定义性特征，是\"因\"不\n是\"果\"——发脾气是这种心态的表现，不是它造成的后果。\nD) 以偏概全\nstir up agitation among those in long lines说敌意\"在排长队的人中引发激动\"——原\n文里long lines只是个举例（举例说明带敌意者的思维特征），并非科学家研究的结论；\n把例子当作结论是典型以偏概全。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What do we learn from the last paragraph about the process of aging?", "options": ["A) It may vary from individual to individual.", "B) It challenges scientists to explore further.", "C) It depends on one's genetic code.", "D) It may be controlled to a degree."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干是回末段问\"我们了解到aging的什么\"，本质上是末段的细节信息题，不是问全文\n主旨。\n【拆句】结构平铺，主干What do we learn，介词短语from the last paragraph限定信息来源，\nabout the process of aging限定话题。无陷阱。\n【定位】What this means: aging is a dynamic process that could possibly be accelerated or slowed—\nand, in some aspects, even reversed.\n这意味着：衰老是一个动态过程，可以被加速或减缓——而且，在某些方面，甚至可以被\n逆转。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nvary from individual to individual说衰老\"因人而异\"——这是首段the differences\nbetween people's rates of aging的话题，不是末段重点；末段讲的是衰老\"可调控\"，\n不是\"个体差异\"。\nB) 无中生有\nchallenges scientists to explore further说衰老\"促使科学家进一步探索\"——末段从未\n表达\"号召科学家继续研究\"的意思，整段是给出已得出的结论。\nC) 与原文相反\ndepends on one's genetic code说衰老\"取决于基因密码\"——但末段telomeres do\nnot simply carry out the commands issued by your genetic code恰好明确反对\"完\n全由基因决定\"的观点；末段的核心就是\"超越基因决定论\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\ncould possibly be accelerated or slowed—and, in some aspects, even reversed ↔\nIt may be controlled to a degree\nYour telomeres are listening to you. ↔ It may be controlled to a degree\n预判\"衰老在某种程度上可被控制\"与It may be controlled to a degree逐词对应；尤其\nmay+to a degree精准对应原文possibly+in some aspects的限定，是六级阅读\"程度\n词不能改\"的典型考察点。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about an enzyme scientists have created?", "options": ["A) It was identified during a lab experiment accident.", "B) It may make full recycling of plastic bottles a reality.", "C) It was a breakthrough made with persistent efforts.", "D) It may initiate a radical reform in plastic industry."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn from the passage about an enzyme scientists have created?\n是 What do we learn about X 的标准客观陈述问法，不含 infer / imply，不问 main idea\n/ attitude，属细节题。\n【拆句】题干主干为 What do we learn from the passage about an enzyme，about 引导的介词\n短语限定 learn 的对象；其后 scientists have created 是省略 that 的定语从句修饰\nenzyme，结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】The breakthrough could help solve the global plastic pollution crisis by enabling for the first\ntime the full recycling of bottles.\n这一突破有望首次实现塑料瓶的完全回收，从而帮助解决全球塑料污染危机。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nidentified during a lab experiment accident 偷换了原文 created by accident——原\n文意思是\"偶然创造出\"，A 项却说成\"在一次实验事故中被识别（identified）出来\"，\nidentified（识别）≠ created（创造），且 lab experiment accident（实验事故）也\n无原文支撑。\nB) ✓ 正确\nenabling for the first time the full recycling of bottles ↔\nmay make full recycling of plastic bottles a reality\nthe full recycling of bottles ↔ full recycling of plastic bottles\nB 项既覆盖\"完全回收\"又保留\"可能性\"语气，与定位句 could help…by enabling for\nthe first time the full recycling of bottles 严格对应，是定位句的同义改写。\nC) 与原文相反\nbreakthrough made with persistent efforts 与原文 created by accident 直接相反；\n同段还点明 a bit of a shock 进一步坐实\"意外性\"。\nD) 无中生有\ninitiate a radical reform in plastic industry 中的 radical reform（根本性改革）在原\n文中无任何对应句；原文谈的是\"帮助解决污染\"和\"完全回收\"，并未谈及行业改革。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the passage say about the bug that produces the important enzyme?", "options": ["A) It has a natural ability to consume plastics.", "B) It is a bacterium that reproduces at a high rate.", "C) It is essential to the recycling of plastic bottles.", "D) It has a chemical structure unknown to scientists."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the passage say about the bug that produces the important\nenzyme? 用 say about 直接索取原文对 the bug 的客观陈述，不含推断词，属细节题。\n【拆句】the bug that produces the important enzyme 中 that 引导的定语从句修饰 bug；六级\n考生需看清 produces 的主语是 the bug 而非更靠近的 enzyme，即所问对象是 bug 本身\n的特征。\n【定位】The new research was spurred by the discovery in 2016 of the first bacterium that had\nnaturally evolved to eat plastic at a waste dump in Japan.\n这项新研究的契机是 2016 年在日本一处垃圾场发现的首个自然进化出能吃塑料能力的\n细菌。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nnaturally evolved to eat plastic ↔ natural ability to consume plastics\nA 项把定位句的动词陈述（自然进化出去吃塑料）改写为名词陈述（具有自然消化塑料\n的能力），是该细菌核心特征的客观转述。\nB) 无中生有\nreproduces at a high rate（高速繁殖）在原文中没有任何对应句；原文只说该细菌\"自\n然进化\"且\"吃塑料\"，没有谈论其繁殖速度。\nC) 偷换概念\nessential to the recycling of plastic bottles 把所问对象（bug 本身的属性）偷换\n成\"对回收的必要性\"——这是对其衍生用途的评价，而非对 bug 本身的描述；且原文也\n未声称 bug 对回收\"必不可少\"。\nD) 与原文相反\nchemical structure unknown to scientists 与原文 Scientists have now revealed the\ndetailed structure of the crucial enzyme produced by the bug. 相反；原文明确\"已\n揭示详细结构\"，选项却说\"科学家不知道\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "By adjusting the enzyme produced by the bug, the scientists_______.", "options": ["A) Made it more effective by chance", "B) Discovered an extraordinary chemical", "C) Altered its basic molecular composition", "D) Found its evolutionary process sped up"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干是不完整陈述 By adjusting the enzyme produced by the bug, the\nscientists_______.，要选项补全，问的是科学家做了什么的客观结果，不含推断动词。\n【拆句】By adjusting the enzyme produced by the bug, the scientists_______. 主干是 the\nscientists ____；By 引导方式状语，produced by the bug 是过去分词短语后置定语修饰\nenzyme，结构清晰。\n【定位】An international team then adjusted the enzyme to see how it had evolved, but tests showed\nthey had accidentally made the molecule even better at breaking down the plastic used for\ndrinks bottles.\n国际团队随后调整了该酶以观察其进化过程，但实验表明他们意外地让这一分子分解饮料瓶\n塑料的能力变得更强了。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\naccidentally made the molecule even better at breaking down the plastic ↔\nmade it more effective by chance\nA 项 made it more effective 复述 even better at breaking down，by chance 复述\naccidentally，正好等值改写定位句两个核心要素。\nB) 无中生有\ndiscovered an extraordinary chemical 中 extraordinary chemical 在原文无出处；研\n究者操作的是已知酶分子，并非\"发现一种新化学物质\"。\nC) 过度推断\naltered its basic molecular composition 中 basic molecular composition（基本分\n子组成）属于结构层级更深的概念，原文只说 adjusted the enzyme（调整酶），未说\n改变其\"基本组成\"。\nD) 偷换概念\nfound its evolutionary process sped up 中 sped up（加速）与原文 to see how\nit had evolved（观察其如何进化）不一致；原文目的是观察，并未发现进化过程被\n加速。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does Prof. McGeehan say about the recycling of plastic bottles?", "options": ["A) Manufacturers are implementing it on an increasingly larger scale.", "B) It generates huge business opportunities for plastic manufacturers.", "C) It has aroused persistent interest among the general public.", "D) Manufacturers are beginning to explore ways of doing it."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does Prof. McGeehan say about the recycling of plastic bottles? 索取\nMcGeehan 关于回收的客观说法，属细节题。\n【拆句】What does Prof. McGeehan say about the recycling of plastic bottles? 标准 Wh- 疑问\n句，about 短语限定 say 的话题对象，无陷阱。\n【定位】perception is changing so much that companies are starting to look at how they can properly\nrecycle these bottles.\n公众观念变化如此之大，以至于企业正开始研究如何妥善回收这些瓶子。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nimplementing it on an increasingly larger scale 中 implementing on a larger scale\n（大规模实施）远超 starting to look at（开始研究）的程度——原文是观念转变后的\n初始阶段，并未谈到任何实施规模。\nB) 无中生有\ngenerates huge business opportunities for plastic manufacturers 中 huge\nbusiness opportunities（巨大商业机会）原文未提；McGeehan 谈的是企业态度转\n变，不是回收带来的商机。\nC) 偷换概念\naroused persistent interest among the general public 中 persistent interest（持续\n/ 长期兴趣）与原文 perception is changing so much（观念正在巨大变化）不一致\n——原文说\"正在变化\"，C 说\"已激起且持续\"；且讨论对象是公众而非回收行为本身，\n与题干 about the recycling of plastic bottles 错位。\nD) ✓ 正确\ncompanies are starting to look at how they can properly recycle these bottles ↔\nManufacturers are beginning to explore ways of doing it\ncompanies ↔ Manufacturers\nD 项把\"才刚开始\"和\"探索如何回收\"两个核心信息都对齐原文，是定位句的精准同义\n改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What is Prof. Adisa Azapagic's advice concerning the application of the enzyme?", "options": ["A) Developing technologies to address greenhouse gas emissions.", "B) Considering the extra cost involved in producing the enzyme.", "C) Assessing its possible negative impact on the environment.", "D) Studying the full life cycle of the enzyme as the first step."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is Prof. Adisa Azapagic's advice concerning the application of the\nenzyme? 索取该专家的具体建议，属细节题。\n【拆句】What is Prof. Adisa Azapagic's advice concerning the application of the enzyme? 中\nconcerning the application of the enzyme 是介词短语修饰 advice，limit 范围为\"针对\n酶的应用\"，结构清晰。\n【定位】A full life-cycle assessment would be needed to ensure that the technology does not solve one\nenvironmental problem—waste—at the expense of others, including additional greenhouse\ngas emissions.\n需要进行一次完整的生命周期评估，以确保该技术不会以制造其他环境问题（包括额外的温\n室气体排放）为代价来解决一个环境问题（废弃物）。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nDeveloping technologies to address greenhouse gas emissions 中 developing\ntechnologies 与原文 assessment（评估）不同——原文建议\"评估\"，并非\"开发新技\n术\"去解决排放问题。\nB) 无中生有\nConsidering the extra cost involved in producing the enzyme 中 extra cost in\nproducing the enzyme（生产酶的额外成本）原文无出处；Adisa Azapagic 的建议是\n评估环境影响而非经济成本。\nC) ✓ 正确\ndoes not solve one environmental problem—waste—at the expense of others, including\nadditional greenhouse gas emissions\n↔ Assessing its possible negative impact on the environment\nA full life-cycle assessment would be needed ↔ Assessing\nC 项的 Assessing 对应 assessment，possible negative impact on the environment\n对应 not solve…at the expense of others, including additional greenhouse gas\nemissions；正答完整覆盖建议的两个语义要点。\nD) 偷换概念\nStudying the full life cycle of the enzyme as the first step 中 the full life cycle of\nthe enzyme（酶本身的生命周期）与原文 A full life-cycle assessment（针对技术应用\n的全生命周期评估）所指不同——原文评估的是技术对环境的全周期影响，并非\"酶的寿\n命周期\"；另外 as the first step（作为第一步）原文亦无对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2022-12-1"});
